Lab 20: Managing Application Deployment Resources in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Upload and manage base/customized images in Glance (OpenStack Image Service).
Define custom flavors to match specific workload requirements.
Configure user data and metadata for instance initialization.
Prerequisites
Access to an OpenStack cloud environment with admin privileges.
OpenStack CLI tools installed (openstack, glance, nova).
Basic familiarity with command-line operations.
A Linux-based base image (e.g., CirrOS, CentOS, or Ubuntu Cloud Image) for download.
Task 1: Upload and Managing Images in Glance
Subtask 1.1: Download a Base Image
Download a base image (e.g., Ubuntu Cloud Image):
wget https://cloud-images.ubuntu.com/focal/current/focal-server-cloudimg-amd64.img
Subtask 1.2: Upload the Image to Glance
Use the openstack CLI to upload the image:

openstack image create "Ubuntu-20.04" \
  --file focal-server-cloudimg-amd64.img \
  --disk-format qcow2 \
  --container-format bare \
  --public
--disk-format qcow2: Specifies the image format.
--public: Makes the image available to all projects.
Verify the upload:

openstack image list
Expected Outcome: The image "Ubuntu-20.04" appears in the list with status active.

Subtask 1.3: Create a Customized Image
Launch a temporary instance using the base image:

openstack server create --image Ubuntu-20.04 --flavor m1.small temp-instance
SSH into the instance, install required packages (e.g., nginx), and power it off:

sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y nginx
sudo poweroff
Create a snapshot:

openstack server image create --name "Ubuntu-20.04-NGINX" temp-instance
Delete the temporary instance:

openstack server delete temp-instance
Task 2: Define Custom Flavors
Subtask 2.1: List Existing Flavors
View available flavors:
openstack flavor list
Subtask 2.2: Create a Custom Flavor
Define a flavor for a memory-intensive workload:

openstack flavor create \
  --ram 8192 \
  --disk 20 \
  --vcpus 4 \
  "mem-heavy"
--ram 8192: 8GB RAM.
--disk 20: 20GB disk space.
--vcpus 4: 4 virtual CPUs.
Verify:

openstack flavor show mem-heavy
Task 3: Configure User Data and Metadata
Subtask 3.1: Launch an Instance with User Data
Create a user-data.txt file for cloud-init:

#!/bin/bash
apt update
apt install -y stress-ng
Launch an instance with the user data:

openstack server create \
  --image Ubuntu-20.04 \
  --flavor mem-heavy \
  --user-data user-data.txt \
  "stress-test-instance"
Subtask 3.2: Attach Metadata
Add metadata to the instance:

openstack server set \
  --property environment=production \
  --property team=devops \
  stress-test-instance
Verify metadata:

openstack server show stress-test-instance
Troubleshooting Tips
Image upload fails: Ensure the image file is not corrupted and the Glance service is running.
Flavor creation denied: Check admin privileges are enabled.
User data not executed: Verify cloud-init is installed in the base image.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Uploaded and customized images in Glance.
Created a custom flavor for memory-heavy workloads.
Configured user data and metadata for instance initialization.
These skills are essential for efficient application deployment in OpenStack environments.


**Note**: All commands use modern OpenStack CLI syntax (Queens release or later) and avoid deprecated technologies. Adjust image names/flavors as per your OpenStack environment.
